      "player": "MiniMx Grudge",
      "decisionIndex": 60
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Used Faith's Fetters (premium removal + 4 life) on a 2/2 Nessian Hornbeetle that isn't currently threatening. Gem3P Coach has a 3/3 flyer that attacks over it, and Angelic Edict in hand for bigger threats. This spent all mana for the turn on neutralizing a small creature.",
      "actionTaken": "Cast Faith's Fetters targeting Nessian Hornbeetle, using all 4 mana.",
      "betterLine": "Cast Angelic Page (2 mana) to add another flyer for aerial pressure alongside Emancipation Angel, keeping 2 mana open. Could also cast both Anointed Chorister and Angelic Page (3 mana total) to develop the board. Save Faith's Fetters/Angelic Edict for larger threats the opponent is likely to deploy.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshotIndex": 64,
      "player": "Gem3P Coach",
      "decisionIndex": 64
    },
    {
      "severity": "questionable",
      "description": "Casting Arbor Armament in precombat main phase rather than holding it. With Primeval Bounty in hand (costing 6 mana), this noncreature spell would generate THREE +1/+1 counters instead of one if cast after Bounty resolves. Additionally, Arbor Armament grants reach until end of turn, making it valuable as a combat trick against the opponent's flying Emancipation Angel — using it precombat wastes the surprise/trick element.",
      "actionTaken": "Cast Arbor Armament precombat on (likely) Nessian Hornbeetle for a permanent +1/+1 counter",
      "betterLine": "Pass and hold Arbor Armament for either a future combat trick (to block Emancipation Angel with reach) or to cast after Primeval Bounty is in play for triple the counter value. Though this was the only legal action, passing (declining to cast) was also an option.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshotIndex": 96,
      "player": "MiniMx Grudge",
      "decisionIndex": 81
    },
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "description": "With 5 mana available and Angelic Edict as a castable option, the player chose to cast Anointed Chorister (a 1/1 lifelink for W) instead of Angelic Edict to exile the 4/4 Nessian Hornbeetle that keeps growing with +1/+1 counters each combat. The Hornbeetle is the opponent's primary threat and will continue to snowball if not removed.",
      "actionTaken": "Cast Anointed Chorister (1/1 lifelink) for W instead of using removal on a growing threat.",
      "betterLine": "Cast Angelic Edict to exile the 4/4 Nessian Hornbeetle, removing the opponent's most dangerous and growing threat.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshotIndex": 112,
      "player": "Gem3P Coach",
      "decisionIndex": 89
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Cast Gaea's Protector before playing the Forest from hand. Should always play land first before casting spells - it's free and ensures you have maximum mana available. While both the land and the spell were 'free' choices here, playing land first is strictly better sequencing. The Forest could have been relevant if anything disrupted lands, and proper sequencing discipline matters.",
      "actionTaken": "Cast Gaea's Protector without first playing the Forest from hand.",
      "betterLine": "Play Forest first (free land drop), then cast Gaea's Protector with the additional mana security. This maintains proper sequencing discipline and leaves no value on the table.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshotIndex": 134,
      "player": "MiniMx Grudge",
      "decisionIndex": 100
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Timed out on Thriving Heath's color choice, likely getting a useless color (Blue or Black) instead of Green. In a G/W deck, the Heath should always choose Green to serve as a dual land. This wastes the land's flexibility and could cause issues casting green spells later.",
      "actionTaken": "Timed out, resulting in a default non-green color choice for Thriving Heath.",
      "betterLine": "Choose Green for Thriving Heath, making it a proper G/W dual land to cast any green spells drawn later.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshotIndex": 157,
      "player": "Gem3P Coach",
      "decisionIndex": 111
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Wildwood Scourge as a 4/4 is a fine body, but Rampaging Growth would have been better here. Rampaging Growth fetches a 6th land (enabling Primeval Bounty next turn - a game-winning card), creates a 4/3 with haste and reach for immediate pressure while the opponent is tapped out, and allows attacking for 8 this turn (Gaea's Protector + 4/3 land). Without ramp, MiniMx Grudge needs to draw a land to ever cast Primeval Bounty.",
      "actionTaken": "Cast Wildwood Scourge for X=4, making a 4/4 without haste",
      "betterLine": "Cast Rampaging Growth to fetch a 6th land, attack with the 4/3 haste land creature and Gaea's Protector for 8 damage while opponent is tapped out, and enable casting Primeval Bounty next turn",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshotIndex": 182,
      "player": "MiniMx Grudge",
      "decisionIndex": 125
    },
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "description": "Opponent is at 6 life with no flying blockers. Emancipation Angel (3/3) and Angelic Page (1/1) are attacking for 4 damage. Casting Basri's Acolyte during combat would put +1/+1 counters on both attacking creatures (making them 4/4 and 2/2), dealing exactly 6 damage for lethal. Instead, Voice of the Provinces uses all mana to create creatures that can't attack this turn, leaving the opponent alive at 2 life.",
      "actionTaken": "Cast Voice of the Provinces (4WW) during combat, which creates a 3/3 flyer and 1/1 token that can't attack this turn.",
      "betterLine": "Cast Basri's Acolyte (2WW) during combat, targeting Emancipation Angel and Angelic Page with the +1/+1 counters. This makes the attackers deal 4+2=6 damage, exactly lethal against opponent at 6 life.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshotIndex": 208,
      "player": "Gem3P Coach",
      "decisionIndex": 140
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "The animated Forest 4/3 (from Rampaging Growth) could attack for free since all opponent's creatures are tapped or have summoning sickness. The Forest will stop being a creature at end of turn anyway, so there's no defensive benefit to holding it back. Missing 4 free damage when at 6 life and needing to race against an opponent at 21 life is a real cost.",
      "actionTaken": "Attacked with only Gaea's Protector and Wildwood Scourge for 7 damage.",
      "betterLine": "Attack with all three creatures (Gaea's Protector, Wildwood Scourge, and the animated Forest) for 11 damage total, since no blockers are available and the Forest loses creature status at end of turn regardless.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshotIndex": 232,
      "player": "MiniMx Grudge",
      "decisionIndex": 150
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Declined to activate Angelic Page to pump an attacker for free. While the attack was already lethal (8 damage vs 6 life), activating Angelic Page is pure upside - it costs nothing (just tapping an untapped non-attacking creature) and provides an extra point of damage as insurance against any potential instant-speed life gain or damage prevention from the opponent's 2 cards in hand.",
      "actionTaken": "Passed without activating Angelic Page's tap ability to give an attacking creature +1/+1.",
      "betterLine": "Activate Angelic Page targeting either attacker to deal 9 instead of 8 damage, providing extra insurance for lethal.",
